1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,117
The final part joins everything to an agent harness. Generative UI becomes just another tool in the loop.

2
00:00:10,117 --> 00:00:23,398
The classic harness loop: call the model, run its tool calls, and append the results. Errors are results too. The loop stops when there are no more tool calls or it hits MAX_TURNS.

3
00:00:23,398 --> 00:00:36,438
render_ui validates the arguments for the current mode: a pre-built component in static, a catalog tree in declarative, policy-checked HTML in open-ended, and exactly one HtmlView in mixed.

4
00:00:36,438 --> 00:00:46,462
The recorded model turns. In turn 2 the model tries a PieChart, which is not in the catalog.

5
00:00:46,462 --> 00:00:55,522
A click on any surface becomes a new user message in the same session, so the model sees the earlier turns.

6
00:00:55,522 --> 00:01:11,627
The terminal surface. In turn 2 the render fails with 'unknown component PieChart'. The model reads that error and corrects it in turn 3.

7
00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:27,835
The web surface shows the same loop. You can see the error, the correction, and then a click that continues the session.

8
00:01:27,835 --> 00:01:34,989
Static: the model only picks sales_overview and fills its props.

9
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:46,156
Open-ended: render_ui with HTML. The harness checks the policy, and the surface sandboxes it.

10
00:01:46,156 --> 00:01:55,322
Mixed: catalog plus one HtmlView, which is the default I would recommend for a real product.

11
00:01:55,322 --> 00:02:04,450
The tests check one render per mode, recovery from the PieChart error, the click continuing the session, and the turn limit.

12
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:16,490
To sum up: pick a format, pick a transport or host, validate everything against the catalog, and keep model-written HTML inside a sandbox. Thanks for watching!

